FENWICK TREE (BINARY INDEXED TREE – BIT)

👉 Asked in advanced interviews, competitive coding, range query problems
👉 Cleaner and faster than Segment Tree for many cases

1️⃣ Problem Statement

You are given an array of integers.
You need to support two operations efficiently:

Update an element at index i

Query the prefix sum from index 0 to i

You must handle both operations in O(log N) time.

Example
Array = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]

PrefixSum(2) → 1 + 3 + 5 = 9
Update index 1 to value 10
New Array = [1, 10, 5, 7, 9]

PrefixSum(2) → 1 + 10 + 5 = 16

2️⃣ Why Fenwick Tree? 💡
Method	Update	Query
Normal Array	O(1)	O(n)
Prefix Sum Array	O(n)	O(1)
Segment Tree	O(log n)	O(log n)
⭐ Fenwick Tree	O(log n)	O(log n)

👉 Fenwick Tree is simpler than Segment Tree

3️⃣ Core Concept (VERY IMPORTANT)

Fenwick Tree uses binary representation of indices

Key operation:
i & (-i)


This:

Gives the last set bit

Decides how far to move in tree

4️⃣ Fenwick Tree Algorithm
➤ Initialization

Use array BIT[] of size n+1 (1-based indexing)

➤ Update(index, delta)

Move to index+1

Add delta to current position

Jump to next responsible index using
i += i & (-i)

➤ Prefix Sum Query(index)

Move to index+1

Add BIT[i] to sum

Move backward using
i -= i & (-i)

5️⃣ Java Code (Interview-Ready & Clean)
class FenwickTree {

    int[] BIT;
    int n;

    // Step 1: Constructor
    public FenwickTree(int n) {
        this.n = n;
        BIT = new int[n + 1]; // 1-based indexing
    }

    // Step 2: Update value at index
    public void update(int index, int value) {

        index++; // convert to 1-based index

        while (index <= n) {
            BIT[index] += value;
            index += index & (-index);
        }
    }

    // Step 3: Prefix Sum Query
    public int query(int index) {

        index++; // convert to 1-based index
        int sum = 0;

        while (index > 0) {
            sum += BIT[index];
            index -= index & (-index);
        }

        return sum;
    }

    // Optional: Range Sum Query
    public int rangeQuery(int left, int right) {
        return query(right) - query(left - 1);
    }
}

6️⃣ Line-by-Line Explanation 🔍
🔹 BIT Array
BIT = new int[n + 1];


Index 0 unused

Makes bit operations easier

🔹 Update Function
index++;


Convert from 0-based to 1-based

BIT[index] += value;


Adds value to responsible nodes

index += index & (-index);


Moves to next index that covers this range

💡 Example:

index = 4 (100)
index & -index = 4
next = 8

🔹 Query Function
sum += BIT[index];


Add partial sum stored at index

index -= index & (-index);


Move to parent node

7️⃣ Example Dry Run 🧠
Initial Array
arr = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]

Build Fenwick Tree
update(0, 1)
update(1, 3)
update(2, 5)
update(3, 7)
update(4, 9)

Query PrefixSum(2)
index = 3
sum += BIT[3] → 5
index = 2
sum += BIT[2] → 4
index = 0 → stop
Total = 9

Update index 1 → value = 10
delta = 10 - 3 = 7
update(1, 7)

New PrefixSum(2)
1 + 10 + 5 = 16

